module hc.selfhost.checker;

// The self-hosted checker, phase 1 -- a genuine type checker (literal/operator/call/assignment
// type rules, a flat function-scoped variable environment, accumulated error messages) walking
// the `Expr`/`Stmt`/`FnDecl` AST `Parser.hotc` produces. Deliberately NOT attempting move/borrow
// checking in this phase -- the real `Checker.kt`'s move-tracking (conservative branch merges,
// capture analysis for lambdas, drop ordering) is a whole separate, much harder feature on top
// of plain type checking, and type checking alone is already a real, self-contained milestone
// worth proving out on its own first. Logged as its own follow-up, not silently skipped.
//
// **Structs now real types**: `Ty` gained a `TyStruct { name }` variant (its first *field-
// carrying* variant -- everything else here is a plain unit tag), backed by a real `structs:
// Registry<StructInfo>` field/type table. `register_struct` (called once per `StructDecl` by the
// driver before any fn body is checked, mirroring how `fn_sigs` gets pre-registered) populates
// it; `FieldAccess`/`FieldAssign`/`StructLit` all resolve against it for real instead of the old
// unconditional `TyUnknown`. Any *other* unrecognized type name (a genuine typo, not a struct)
// now also becomes a `TyStruct` tag rather than the old silently-trusted `TyUnknown` -- surfacing
// it as a real mismatch wherever it's used inconsistently, which is strictly more correct, not a
// scope cut.
//
// **Struct methods now real too**: a flat `methods: Registry<FnSig>` (keyed `"StructName#method"`)
// backs `register_method`/`check_method` (called once per impl-block method, mirroring
// `register_struct`) -- `check_method` binds `self` into the variable environment (the struct's
// own type) before checking params/body. `check_method_call` resolves against this table first,
// falling back to the old unconditional `TyUnknown` only for receivers with no registered method
// by that name (covers `String`'s real built-in methods, resolved concretely only in
// `Codegen.hotc`, and genuine typos alike -- a real, disclosed imprecision, not a false-negative
// risk: it costs a missing error message on a typo, never rejects something that should compile).
// Nested struct-typed fields and struct-typed fn params/return types both already work (verified
// in `Codegen.hotc`, not a checker-side change -- `ty_from_name`'s `TyStruct` fallback and
// `find_field_type`'s lookups are already fully general).
//
// **Scope cut, still disclosed**: no interface types. No block-scoping -- variables live in one
// flat, whole-function table; a shadowed `let` inside an `if`/`while` silently reuses the same
// slot rather than properly scoping, since `Registry<T>.get()` returns the *first* match, not the
// most recent. (Enums and static `impl` methods are real now -- see below.)
//
// **Generic enums now real too** (`enum Option<T> { ... }`, mirroring generic structs'
// `Base$Arg`-name on-demand monomorphization): `GenericEnumTemplate` parallels `GenericTemplate`,
// `register_enum` gained a `type_param` param branching into it exactly like `register_struct`
// does, and `ensure_instantiated` gained a second lookup (struct templates, then enum templates)
// substituting `T` across every variant's fields. The one genuinely new problem generic enums
// raise that generic structs don't: a *plain* generic struct's own concrete name already IS its
// instantiation name (`Box<Int> { ... }` directly constructs `"Box$Int"`), but an enum variant's
// bare name (`"Some"`) carries no such information -- two different concrete instantiations of the
// same generic enum used in one program (`Option<Int>` and `Option<String>`) would silently
// collide in the old bare-name-keyed `variant_owner` table (`Registry<T>.get()` returns the FIRST
// match, no shadowing). Fixed two ways: (1) construction sites for a generic enum's variants must
// use the explicit qualified form `Base<Arg>::Variant { ... }` (parsed into `Expr::StructLit`'s
// new `generic_base` field, `Ast.hc`), which `check_struct_lit` resolves directly, bypassing
// `variant_owner` entirely; (2) `check_match`'s variant-ownership check now consults a new
// `variant_owner_q: Registry<Bool>` keyed `"Owner#Variant"` (populated for every enum, plain and
// generic alike) instead of the bare table, since `match` already identifies the subject's own
// concrete type name with no ambiguity. Real bug found building this: `check_match` never called
// `ensure_instantiated` on its subject's type (unlike `check_field_access`/`check_method_call`,
// which already did) -- a generic-enum-typed match subject in a function checked *before* any
// function that actually constructs that instantiation failed every arm with a spurious "'Some' is
// not a variant of 'Option$Int'", since nothing had monomorphized it yet. Fixed by adding the same
// `ensure_instantiated` call `check_match` was missing. Bare unqualified construction (`Some {
// ... }`) still resolves fine for a *non*-generic enum's variants, unchanged.
//
// **Real finding building this file**: `Ty` (an enum) isn't `Copy` -- comparing the SAME value
// against multiple candidates (`if lt == TyInt ... else if lt == TyUnknown ...`) moves it on the
// first comparison, making the second a compile error. The clean fix, used throughout below: call
// `ty_name(t)` (returning a `String`, which IS `Copy`) exactly once per `Ty` value, then do every
// subsequent comparison against that name instead of the enum value itself.

// `Ty`/`FnSig`/`StructInfo`/`GenericTemplate`/`GenericEnumTemplate`/`ExternMethodInfo`, plus
// `ty_from_name`/`ty_name`/`empty_ty_vec`/`param_types_of`, now all live in `Types.hotc` --
// shared with `Codegen.hotc`, which needs this same vocabulary to re-derive a generic-fn
// instantiation's own signature (see that file's own header). Split out for the same single-
// responsibility reason `Ast.hotc`/`Lexer.hotc`/`Parser.hotc` are already their own files: this
// file owns the type-CHECKING algorithm, not the definition of what a type IS.

pub struct TyChecker {
    fn_sigs: Registry<FnSig>,
    vars: Registry<Ty>,
    structs: Registry<StructInfo>,
    // Keyed by "StructName#methodName" -- a flat map instead of a nested per-struct one, same
    // simplification `find_field_type`'s linear scan makes for field lookups.
    methods: Registry<FnSig>,
    // variant name -> owning enum name. An enum's OWN field table is registered into `structs`
    // under the enum's own name (the union of every variant's fields, see `register_enum`) --
    // `structs` doesn't distinguish "real struct" from "enum, field-lookup purposes" at all, only
    // this table does, and only for telling a `StructLit` construction site (`Name { ... }`)
    // whether `Name` is a plain struct or an enum variant.
    variant_owner: Registry<String>,
    // "OwnerName#VariantName" -> existence, populated for EVERY enum (plain and generic mono
    // instantiations alike) -- `check_match`'s own variant-ownership check uses this instead of
    // `variant_owner` above, since `variant_owner` alone would collide across multiple concrete
    // instantiations of the same generic enum (see `Ast.hc`'s own `EnumDecl` header).
    variant_owner_q: Registry<Bool>,
    generic_templates: Registry<GenericTemplate>,
    generic_enum_templates: Registry<GenericEnumTemplate>,
    // Every monomorphized instantiation name (`"Box$Int"`) actually constructed/accessed while
    // checking the program, in first-use order -- `compile_program` reads this back afterward to
    // know exactly which concrete classes `gen_struct` needs to emit (see `Codegen.hotc`'s own
    // header), since nothing else records "which instantiations does this program really use."
    mono_needed: Vec<String>,
    // extern class alias name -> its real binary name (existence check only -- `Codegen.hotc`
    // needs the actual binary name too, but rebuilds that table itself from the same original
    // `externs: Vec<ExternClassDecl>` rather than reading it back from here, same pattern structs/
    // methods/enums already use).
    externs: Registry<String>,
    // Keyed by "Alias#methodName" -- covers constructors and static methods (`Expr::StaticCall`)
    // and instance methods (`Expr::MethodCall`) alike.
    extern_methods: Registry<ExternMethodInfo>,
    // Enum name -> its own variant names, declaration order -- `check_match` reads this to check
    // exhaustiveness (every variant covered by name, or a `_` wildcard arm present).
    enum_variants: Registry<Vec<String>>,
    // Keyed by "StructName#methodName", same flat convention `methods` (instance) uses -- called
    // via `Expr::StaticCall` (`StructName::method(args)`), extern classes checked first, this as
    // the fallback -- see `check_static_call`.
    static_methods: Registry<FnSig>,
    // A generic top-level fn declaration (`fn identity<T>(x: T) -> T { ... }`), kept separate from
    // `fn_sigs` (real, directly-callable signatures) until some call site's inferred type argument
    // asks for a concrete instantiation -- mirrors `generic_templates`/`generic_enum_templates`,
    // except the type argument is INFERRED at each call site rather than spelled out (see
    // `check_call`'s own header).
    generic_fn_templates: Registry<FnDecl>,
    // Every monomorphized FUNCTION instantiation name (`"identity$Int"`) actually called while
    // checking the program, in first-use order -- mirrors `mono_needed`, read back by
    // `compile_program` afterward to know which concrete fn bodies `Codegen.hotc` needs to emit.
    fn_mono_needed: Vec<String>,
    // An instance method declared inside a GENERIC struct's own `impl` block (`impl Box<T> { fn
    // get(&self) -> T { ... } }`), keyed "StructName#methodName" like `methods` above -- kept
    // separate until some method-call site's RECEIVER (already a concrete `"Box$Int"`-shaped
    // instantiation) asks for it. Unlike a generic top-level fn's own call-site inference (scanning
    // params for one whose type is bare `T`), a generic method's `T` needs no scanning at all --
    // the receiver's own mono name already names it directly (`mono_arg("Box$Int")` = `"Int"`), see
    // `ensure_method_instantiated`. Scope cut, disclosed: only instance methods (`&self`/`&mut
    // self`) get this treatment -- a `static fn` inside a generic struct's `impl` block is a rarer
    // shape (the real prelude's own `Vec<T>`/`Registry<T>` don't use one) and stays unsupported.
    generic_method_templates: Registry<FnDecl>,
    // Every monomorphized METHOD instantiation key (`"Box$Int#get"`) actually called while checking
    // the program -- mirrors `fn_mono_needed`, read back by `compile_program` afterward to know
    // which concrete method bodies `Codegen.hotc` needs to emit onto their owning mono struct's own
    // class file (not the main program class, unlike a plain generic fn -- see `Codegen.hotc`'s own
    // header).
    method_mono_needed: Vec<String>,
    // Top-level `pub static NAME: Type = expr;` name -> declared type -- unlike `vars` (reset
    // fresh per fn/method), this is registered once up front and stays visible everywhere, real
    // global-constant semantics. `check_ident` checks `vars` first so a local can still shadow a
    // same-named static.
    statics: Registry<Ty>,
    errors: Vec<String>,
    // How many `while` bodies deep the checker currently is -- `Break`/`Continue` are only legal
    // while this is > 0. Incremented/decremented around a `While`'s own body check; no stack of
    // labels needed since this subset never needs to know WHICH enclosing loop, only whether one
    // exists at all.
    loop_depth: Int,
    // The fn/method currently being checked own declared return type name (set fresh at the top
    // of `check_fn`/`check_method`, mirrors `Codegen.hotc`'s own `ret_ty` field) -- read by
    // `check_stmt`'s `Return` arm to supply `check_struct_lit`'s `expected_mono` hint for a bare
    // generic struct literal (`return Vec { ... };`, see that fn's own header).
    cur_ret_ty: String,
    // Bare variant name -> THAT variant's own field table alone (not the enum-wide union
    // `structs` registers it under). Real gap found self-compiling `Parser.hotc`/`Ast.hotc`:
    // `structs.register(enumName, ...)` concatenates EVERY variant's fields into one flat table
    // (disclosed already -- "field lookups... don't distinguish which variant a field actually
    // belongs" -- this file's own header), so `find_field_type`'s linear scan returns the FIRST
    // same-named field it finds across the WHOLE enum, regardless of which variant is actually
    // being constructed. Harmless as long as no two variants reuse a field name at different
    // types -- exactly what `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr` enum does (`IntLit.value: Int`, `Assign.value:
    // Expr`, `StringLit.value: String`, ...), so every one of those was silently validated
    // against `IntLit`'s `Int` (registered first) until this. `check_struct_lit` prefers this
    // table over the enum-wide union whenever the name being constructed is a known variant.
    variant_field_info: Registry<StructInfo>,
    // Bare variant name -> owning GENERIC enum's own BASE name (e.g. "Some"/"None" -> "Option"),
    // registered once at DECLARATION time (`register_enum`'s `is_generic` branch) -- unlike
    // `variant_owner` (which only ever learns about a generic enum's variants AFTER some concrete
    // instantiation happens, via `ensure_instantiated`'s own call back into `register_enum`), this
    // is populated immediately, so a bare `Some { ... }`/`None` construction site (real
    // `Parser.hotc` usage -- `none_expr`/`some_expr`-style helpers, all in return position) can
    // resolve its OWNER even before anything has triggered the FIRST-EVER instantiation of
    // `Option<T>` anywhere in the program. See `check_struct_lit`'s own header for how the actual
    // type argument then gets filled in (from `expected_mono`).
    generic_variant_owner: Registry<String>,
}

pub fn checker_new() -> TyChecker {
    var sigs = registry_new("__init__", FnSig { param_types: empty_ty_vec(), ret_type: TyUnknown });
    var vars = registry_new("__init__", TyUnknown);
    var structs = registry_new("__init__", StructInfo { field_names: empty_str_vec2(), field_types: empty_str_vec2() });
    var methods = registry_new("__init__", FnSig { param_types: empty_ty_vec(), ret_type: TyUnknown });
    var variant_owner = registry_new("__init__", "__init__");
    var variant_owner_q = registry_new("__init__", true);
    var generic_templates = registry_new("__init__", GenericTemplate { type_param: "__init__", field_names: empty_str_vec2(), field_types: empty_str_vec2() });
    var generic_enum_templates = registry_new("__init__", GenericEnumTemplate { type_param: "__init__", variants: empty_variant_vec3() });
    var externs = registry_new("__init__", "__init__");
    var extern_methods = registry_new("__init__", ExternMethodInfo { param_types: empty_str_vec2(), ret_type: "Unit", is_static: false, is_ctor: false });
    var enum_variants = registry_new("__init__", empty_str_vec2());
    var static_methods = registry_new("__init__", FnSig { param_types: empty_ty_vec(), ret_type: TyUnknown });
    var generic_fn_templates = registry_new("__init__", FnDecl { name: "__init__", params: empty_param_vec3(), ret_type: none_string3(), body: empty_stmt_vec3(), type_param: none_string3() });
    var generic_method_templates = registry_new("__init__", FnDecl { name: "__init__", params: empty_param_vec3(), ret_type: none_string3(), body: empty_stmt_vec3(), type_param: none_string3() });
    var mono = vec_of("__init__");
    var mono_needed = Vec { data: mono.data, len: 0 };
    var fmono = vec_of("__init__");
    var fn_mono_needed = Vec { data: fmono.data, len: 0 };
    var mmono = vec_of("__init__");
    var method_mono_needed = Vec { data: mmono.data, len: 0 };
    var statics = registry_new("__init__", TyUnknown);
    var errs = vec_of("__init__");
    errs.set(0, "");
    var variant_field_info = registry_new("__init__", StructInfo { field_names: empty_str_vec2(), field_types: empty_str_vec2() });
    var generic_variant_owner = registry_new("__init__", "__init__");
    return TyChecker { fn_sigs: sigs, vars: vars, structs: structs, methods: methods, variant_owner: variant_owner, variant_owner_q: variant_owner_q, generic_templates: generic_templates, generic_enum_templates: generic_enum_templates, mono_needed: mono_needed, externs: externs, extern_methods: extern_methods, enum_variants: enum_variants, static_methods: static_methods, generic_fn_templates: generic_fn_templates, fn_mono_needed: fn_mono_needed, generic_method_templates: generic_method_templates, method_mono_needed: method_mono_needed, statics: statics, errors: errs, loop_depth: 0, cur_ret_ty: "Unit", variant_field_info: variant_field_info, generic_variant_owner: generic_variant_owner };
}

fn empty_str_vec2() -> Vec<String> {
    var v = vec_of("__init__");
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

fn param_type_names_of4(params: Vec<Param>) -> Vec<String> {
    var out = empty_str_vec2();
    var i = 0;
    while i < params.length() {
        out.push(params.get(i).type_name);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

fn opt_string_or2(o: Option<String>, default_val: String) -> String {
    if let Some { value: v } = o {
        return v;
    }
    return default_val;
}

// Linear scan for a field's declared type name, "?" if the struct has no such field -- mirrors
// `Ty`'s own "?" sentinel for "unknown, don't error on it" so callers can reuse the same
// `!= "?"` guard pattern already established throughout this file.
fn find_field_type(info: StructInfo, field: String) -> String {
    var i = 0;
    while i < info.field_names.length() {
        if info.field_names.get(i) == field {
            return info.field_types.get(i);
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return "?";
}

extern class JStringChk = "java.lang.String" {
    fn length(&self) -> Int;
    fn codePointAt(&self, index: Int) -> Int;
    fn substring(&self, start: Int, end: Int) -> String;
    fn indexOf(&self, s: String) -> Int;
}

// Monomorphized generic instantiations are named `"Base$Arg"` -- `$` specifically because it can
// never appear in a real HC identifier (see `Parser.hotc`'s `type_name_ref` header), so this
// split is unambiguous with any real user-written name.
fn is_mono_name(name: String) -> Bool {
    return name.indexOf("$") >= 0;
}

fn mono_base(name: String) -> String {
    let idx = name.indexOf("$");
    return name.substring(0, idx);
}

fn mono_arg(name: String) -> String {
    let idx = name.indexOf("$");
    return name.substring(idx + 1, name.length());
}

fn empty_param_vec3() -> Vec<Param> {
    var v = vec_of(Param { name: "_", type_name: "_" });
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

fn empty_variant_vec3() -> Vec<EnumVariant> {
    var v = vec_of(EnumVariant { name: "_", fields: empty_param_vec3() });
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

fn none_string3() -> Option<String> {
    return None;
}

fn some_string3(s: String) -> Option<String> {
    return Some { value: s };
}

fn empty_stmt_vec3() -> Vec<Stmt> {
    var v = vec_of(ExprStmt { expr: IntLit { value: 0 } });
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

// Array types are represented as the plain string `"[ElemType]"` throughout this file (see
// `Parser.hotc`'s own `type_name_ref` header) -- these two helpers are the only place that
// convention actually gets parsed back apart.
fn is_array_type_name(name: String) -> Bool {
    if name.length() < 2 { return false; }
    return name.codePointAt(0) == 91 && name.codePointAt(name.length() - 1) == 93; // '[' / ']'
}

fn array_elem_type_name(name: String) -> String {
    return name.substring(1, name.length() - 1);
}

// Substitutes a generic template's type parameter into one declared field type -- handles the
// plain exact-match case (`ftype == "T"` -> the concrete arg, e.g. `"Int"`), an array-of-the-type-
// parameter (`ftype == "[T]"` -> `"[Int]"`, needed for `Vec<T> { data: [T], len: Int }`'s own
// `data` field), AND, new this phase, a *different* generic instantiation naming the type
// parameter as its own sole type argument (`ftype == "Vec$T"` -> `"Vec$Int"`, needed for
// `Registry<T> { entries: Vec<T> }`'s own `entries` field -- see `ensure_instantiated`'s own
// header for the cascading half of this: substituting the field type alone isn't enough, the
// newly-produced nested name has to actually get monomorphized too). Scope cut, still disclosed:
// no deeper nesting than one level (`[[T]]`, or a field type that names `T` somewhere inside a
// *compound* type argument rather than as the argument in its entirety, e.g. a hypothetical
// `Vec<Box<T>>`, isn't substituted here -- `type_name_ref`'s own single-bare-identifier-argument
// scope cut means a generic type argument that's itself generic can't even be parsed yet, so this
// case can't arise from real source today regardless).
// Real bug found stress-testing this against a closer port of the real prelude's own
// `Registry<T> { entries: Vec<RegistryEntry<T>> }`: the ORIGINAL version of this function only
// substituted ONE level of mono-name nesting (`mono_arg(ftype) == type_param` required the entire
// argument portion to be the bare type parameter) -- `"Vec$RegistryEntry$T"` (three levels: `T`
// is the OUTER instantiation's argument's OWN argument) fell through completely unchanged, since
// `mono_arg("Vec$RegistryEntry$T")` is `"RegistryEntry$T"`, not `"T"`. Fixed by recursing into a
// mono-name's own argument portion instead of demanding an exact match there -- this strictly
// generalizes the old single-level check (recursing on a bare `"T"` just hits the exact-match
// branch immediately and returns `arg`, so every previously-working case behaves identically) while
// correctly handling arbitrarily deep chains: `substitute_field_type("Vec$RegistryEntry$T", "T",
// "Int")` now recurses into `"RegistryEntry$T"` -> `"RegistryEntry$Int"`, then wraps back into
// `"Vec$RegistryEntry$Int"`.
fn substitute_field_type(ftype: String, type_param: String, arg: String) -> String {
    if ftype == type_param {
        return arg;
    }
    if is_array_type_name(ftype) && array_elem_type_name(ftype) == type_param {
        return "[" + arg + "]";
    }
    if is_mono_name(ftype) {
        return mono_base(ftype) + "$" + substitute_field_type(mono_arg(ftype), type_param, arg);
    }
    return ftype;
}

// A substituted field type's own CASCADE target -- what `ensure_instantiated` should actually try
// to instantiate next. Real bug found alongside the nested-mono-name one above: `is_mono_name`
// just checks for `"$"` ANYWHERE in the string, so `"[RegistryEntry$Int]"` (an array-of-mono-name
// field, e.g. `Vec<T> { data: [T] }`'s own `data` after `T` substitutes to a mono name) reads as a
// mono name ITSELF -- feeding the whole bracketed string into `ensure_instantiated` would try to
// `mono_base`/`mono_arg` split `"[RegistryEntry$Int]"` (garbage: `"[RegistryEntry"`/`"Int]"`),
// silently no-op (neither `generic_templates` nor `generic_enum_templates` has an entry under that
// garbage base), and never actually cascade into `"RegistryEntry$Int"` at all. Unwrapping one
// array level first (the same "single array level" scope cut already disclosed elsewhere) fixes
// it: an array field's cascade target is its ELEMENT type, not the bracketed string itself.
fn cascade_target(ftype: String) -> String {
    if is_array_type_name(ftype) {
        return array_elem_type_name(ftype);
    }
    return ftype;
}

fn empty_expr_vec3() -> Vec<Expr> {
    var v = vec_of(IntLit { value: 0 });
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

fn empty_matcharm_vec3() -> Vec<MatchArm> {
    var v = vec_of(MatchArm { variant_name: "_", field_names: empty_str_vec2(), bind_names: empty_str_vec2(), body: empty_stmt_vec3() });
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

// A real, whole-tree copy of `e`, substituting `type_param` for `arg` everywhere a TYPE NAME
// STRING appears inside it -- the only two spots that happens in this AST are `StructLit`'s own
// `name`/`generic_base` (`substitute_field_type` handles the same three shapes `ensure_fn_
// instantiated`'s param/return substitution already does: bare `"T"`, `"[T]"`, or a *different*
// generic instantiation naming `T` as its own sole argument, `"Vec$T"`) and `StaticCall`'s
// `type_name` (a generic struct's own static method, `T::wrap(x)`-shaped, though this can't arise
// from real source yet since only enum/struct construction sites currently support the qualified
// `Base<Arg>` form -- included for completeness/robustness regardless). This is the piece that
// closes the gap disclosed in `ensure_fn_instantiated`'s own header: a generic fn's body CAN now
// reference its own still-abstract `T` inside a nested generic construction site (`vec_of<T>`'s
// own `return Vec<T> { data: [first; 4], len: 1 };`, say) -- every other `Expr`/`Stmt` variant
// here just recurses into its own sub-expressions unchanged, no type name of its own to fix up.
fn subst_expr(e: Expr, type_param: String, arg: String) -> Expr {
    match e {
        IntLit { value } => { return IntLit { value: value }; }
        StringLit { value } => { return StringLit { value: value }; }
        BoolLit { value } => { return BoolLit { value: value }; }
        Ident { name } => { return Ident { name: name }; }
        Binary { op, left, right } => { return Binary { op: op, left: subst_expr(left, type_param, arg), right: subst_expr(right, type_param, arg) }; }
        Unary { op, expr } => { return Unary { op: op, expr: subst_expr(expr, type_param, arg) }; }
        Call { callee, args } => { return Call { callee: callee, args: subst_expr_list(args, type_param, arg) }; }
        FieldAccess { obj, field } => { return FieldAccess { obj: subst_expr(obj, type_param, arg), field: field }; }
        MethodCall { recv, method, args } => { return MethodCall { recv: subst_expr(recv, type_param, arg), method: method, args: subst_expr_list(args, type_param, arg) }; }
        Assign { name, value } => { return Assign { name: name, value: subst_expr(value, type_param, arg) }; }
        FieldAssign { obj, field, value } => { return FieldAssign { obj: subst_expr(obj, type_param, arg), field: field, value: subst_expr(value, type_param, arg) }; }
        StructLit { name, field_names, field_values, generic_base } => {
            let new_name = substitute_field_type(name, type_param, arg);
            var new_gb = none_string3();
            if let Some { value: gb } = generic_base {
                new_gb = some_string3(substitute_field_type(gb, type_param, arg));
            }
            return StructLit { name: new_name, field_names: field_names, field_values: subst_expr_list(field_values, type_param, arg), generic_base: new_gb };
        }
        ArrayLit { elements } => { return ArrayLit { elements: subst_expr_list(elements, type_param, arg) }; }
        ArrayRepeat { value, count } => { return ArrayRepeat { value: subst_expr(value, type_param, arg), count: subst_expr(count, type_param, arg) }; }
        Index { arr, index } => { return Index { arr: subst_expr(arr, type_param, arg), index: subst_expr(index, type_param, arg) }; }
        IndexAssign { arr, index, value } => { return IndexAssign { arr: subst_expr(arr, type_param, arg), index: subst_expr(index, type_param, arg), value: subst_expr(value, type_param, arg) }; }
        StaticCall { type_name, method, args } => { return StaticCall { type_name: substitute_field_type(type_name, type_param, arg), method: method, args: subst_expr_list(args, type_param, arg) }; }
        Cast { expr, target_type } => { return Cast { expr: subst_expr(expr, type_param, arg), target_type: target_type }; }
    }
}

fn subst_expr_list(es: Vec<Expr>, type_param: String, arg: String) -> Vec<Expr> {
    var out = empty_expr_vec3();
    var i = 0;
    while i < es.length() {
        out.push(subst_expr(es.get(i), type_param, arg));
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

fn subst_stmt(s: Stmt, type_param: String, arg: String) -> Stmt {
    match s {
        Let { name, mutable, init } => { return Let { name: name, mutable: mutable, init: subst_expr(init, type_param, arg) }; }
        ExprStmt { expr } => { return ExprStmt { expr: subst_expr(expr, type_param, arg) }; }
        Return { value } => {
            var new_val = none_expr3();
            if let Some { value: v } = value { new_val = some_expr3(subst_expr(v, type_param, arg)); }
            return Return { value: new_val };
        }
        If { cond, then_body, else_body } => {
            var new_else = none_stmt_list3();
            if let Some { value: eb } = else_body { new_else = some_stmt_list3(subst_stmt_list(eb, type_param, arg)); }
            return If { cond: subst_expr(cond, type_param, arg), then_body: subst_stmt_list(then_body, type_param, arg), else_body: new_else };
        }
        While { cond, body } => { return While { cond: subst_expr(cond, type_param, arg), body: subst_stmt_list(body, type_param, arg) }; }
        Match { subject, arms } => { return Match { subject: subst_expr(subject, type_param, arg), arms: subst_arm_list(arms, type_param, arg) }; }
        Throw { value } => { return Throw { value: subst_expr(value, type_param, arg) }; }
        Break {} => { return Break {}; }
        Continue {} => { return Continue {}; }
        Try { try_block, catch_var, catch_type, catch_block } => {
            return Try { try_block: subst_stmt_list(try_block, type_param, arg), catch_var: catch_var, catch_type: catch_type, catch_block: subst_stmt_list(catch_block, type_param, arg) };
        }
    }
}

fn subst_stmt_list(ss: Vec<Stmt>, type_param: String, arg: String) -> Vec<Stmt> {
    var out = empty_stmt_vec3();
    var i = 0;
    while i < ss.length() {
        out.push(subst_stmt(ss.get(i), type_param, arg));
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

fn subst_arm_list(arms: Vec<MatchArm>, type_param: String, arg: String) -> Vec<MatchArm> {
    var out = empty_matcharm_vec3();
    var i = 0;
    while i < arms.length() {
        let a = arms.get(i);
        out.push(MatchArm { variant_name: a.variant_name, field_names: a.field_names, bind_names: a.bind_names, body: subst_stmt_list(a.body, type_param, arg) });
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

fn none_expr3() -> Option<Expr> {
    return None;
}

fn some_expr3(e: Expr) -> Option<Expr> {
    return Some { value: e };
}

fn none_stmt_list3() -> Option<Vec<Stmt>> {
    return None;
}

fn some_stmt_list3(ss: Vec<Stmt>) -> Option<Vec<Stmt>> {
    return Some { value: ss };
}

impl TyChecker {
    fn error(&mut self, msg: String) {
        self.errors.push(msg);
    }

    // === Expressions ===

    fn check_expr(&mut self, e: Expr) -> Ty {
        match e {
            IntLit { value } => { return TyInt; }
            StringLit { value } => { return TyStr; }
            BoolLit { value } => { return TyBool; }
            Ident { name } => { return self.check_ident(name); }
            Binary { op, left, right } => { return self.check_binary(op, left, right); }
            Unary { op, expr } => { return self.check_unary(op, expr); }
            Call { callee, args } => { return self.check_call(callee, args); }
            FieldAccess { obj, field } => { return self.check_field_access(obj, field); }
            MethodCall { recv, method, args } => { return self.check_method_call(recv, method, args); }
            Assign { name, value } => { return self.check_assign(name, value); }
            FieldAssign { obj, field, value } => { return self.check_field_assign(obj, field, value); }
            // `self.cur_ret_ty` is passed unconditionally as `expected_mono` -- `check_struct_lit`
            // only ever CONSULTS it as a fallback when the bare struct name doesn't resolve on its
            // own (a real, non-generic struct, or an already-mono generic name like "Vec$T"), so
            // passing it here even for expressions that aren't actually in return position is
            // harmless: it's simply ignored whenever it isn't needed. See that fn's own header --
            // real gap found self-compiling `Parser.hotc`, whose own helper fns all write `return
            // Vec { ... };` inside a `-> Vec<X>` fn.
            StructLit { name, field_names, field_values, generic_base } => { return self.check_struct_lit(name, field_names, field_values, generic_base, some_string3(self.cur_ret_ty)); }
            ArrayLit { elements } => { return self.check_array_lit(elements); }
            ArrayRepeat { value, count } => { return self.check_array_repeat(value, count); }
            Index { arr, index } => { return self.check_index(arr, index); }
            IndexAssign { arr, index, value } => { return self.check_index_assign(arr, index, value); }
            StaticCall { type_name, method, args } => { return self.check_static_call(type_name, method, args); }
            Cast { expr, target_type } => { return self.check_cast(expr, target_type); }
        }
    }

    // `expr as Type` -- `target_type` is trusted at face value, same "declared type is truth"
    // honesty every other declared type already gets in this checker (see `Ast.hc`'s own `Cast`
    // header for why cast-legality checking is out of scope this round). `expr` is still checked
    // for real (its own errors, e.g. an unknown variable inside it, still surface).
    fn check_cast(&mut self, expr: Expr, target_type: String) -> Ty {
        self.check_expr(expr);
        return ty_from_name(target_type);
    }

    fn check_static_call(&mut self, type_name: String, method: String, args: Vec<Expr>) -> Ty {
        var arg_tys = self.check_arg_list(args);
        let key = type_name + "#" + method;
        let cls_found = self.externs.get(type_name);
        var cls_known = false;
        if let Some { value: b } = cls_found { cls_known = true; }
        if cls_known {
            let m_found = self.extern_methods.get(key);
            if let Some { value: m } = m_found {
                let expected_count = m.param_types.length();
                let got_count = arg_tys.length();
                if expected_count != got_count {
                    self.error("'" + method + "' expects " + JString::valueOf(expected_count) + " args, got " + JString::valueOf(got_count));
                } else {
                    var i = 0;
                    while i < got_count {
                        let expected = m.param_types.get(i);
                        let got = arg_tys.get(i);
                        let gn = ty_name(got);
                        if expected != "?" && gn != "?" && expected != gn {
                            self.error("argument " + JString::valueOf(i + 1) + " to '" + method + "' expects " + expected + ", got " + gn);
                        }
                        i = i + 1;
                    }
                }
                if m.is_ctor { return ty_from_name(type_name); }
                if m.ret_type == "Self" { return ty_from_name(type_name); }
                return ty_from_name(m.ret_type);
            } else {
                self.error("unknown method '" + method + "' on extern class '" + type_name + "'");
                return TyUnknown;
            }
        }
        // Not a known extern class -- fall back to a struct static method.
        let sm_found = self.static_methods.get(key);
        if let Some { value: sig } = sm_found {
            let expected_count = sig.param_types.length();
            let got_count = arg_tys.length();
            if expected_count != got_count {
                self.error("'" + method + "' expects " + JString::valueOf(expected_count) + " args, got " + JString::valueOf(got_count));
            } else {
                var i = 0;
                while i < got_count {
                    let expected = sig.param_types.get(i);
                    let got = arg_tys.get(i);
                    let en = ty_name(expected);
                    let gn = ty_name(got);
                    if en != "?" && gn != "?" && en != gn {
                        self.error("argument " + JString::valueOf(i + 1) + " to '" + method + "' expects " + en + ", got " + gn);
                    }
                    i = i + 1;
                }
            }
            return sig.ret_type;
        }
        self.error("unknown extern class or struct '" + type_name + "' (no static method '" + method + "')");
        return TyUnknown;
    }

    fn check_array_lit(&mut self, elements: Vec<Expr>) -> Ty {
        if elements.length() == 0 {
            self.error("empty array literal not supported (element type can't be inferred)");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        let first_ty = self.check_expr(elements.get(0));
        let first_name = ty_name(first_ty);
        var i = 1;
        while i < elements.length() {
            let t = self.check_expr(elements.get(i));
            let n = ty_name(t);
            if n != "?" && first_name != "?" && n != first_name {
                self.error("array elements must all have the same type, expected " + first_name + ", got " + n);
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return TyStruct { name: "[" + first_name + "]" };
    }

    // `[value; count]` -- `count` must be `Int`, and the element type is just `value`'s own type
    // (no same-type-as-siblings check needed, unlike `check_array_lit` -- there's only ever one
    // value expression here, re-evaluated at runtime, not a list of distinct ones to compare).
    fn check_array_repeat(&mut self, value: Expr, count: Expr) -> Ty {
        let vt = self.check_expr(value);
        let vn = ty_name(vt);
        let ct = self.check_expr(count);
        let cn = ty_name(ct);
        if cn != "Int" && cn != "?" {
            self.error("array repeat count must be Int, got " + cn);
        }
        return TyStruct { name: "[" + vn + "]" };
    }

    fn check_index(&mut self, arr: Expr, index: Expr) -> Ty {
        let at = self.check_expr(arr);
        let an = ty_name(at);
        let it = self.check_expr(index);
        let in_ = ty_name(it);
        if in_ != "Int" && in_ != "?" {
            self.error("array index must be Int, got " + in_);
        }
        if an == "?" { return TyUnknown; }
        if !is_array_type_name(an) {
            self.error("type '" + an + "' is not an array, can't index it");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        return ty_from_name(array_elem_type_name(an));
    }

    fn check_index_assign(&mut self, arr: Expr, index: Expr, value: Expr) -> Ty {
        let at = self.check_expr(arr);
        let an = ty_name(at);
        let it = self.check_expr(index);
        let in_ = ty_name(it);
        if in_ != "Int" && in_ != "?" {
            self.error("array index must be Int, got " + in_);
        }
        let vt = self.check_expr(value);
        let vn = ty_name(vt);
        if an == "?" { return ty_from_name(vn); }
        if !is_array_type_name(an) {
            self.error("type '" + an + "' is not an array, can't index it");
            return ty_from_name(vn);
        }
        let elem = array_elem_type_name(an);
        if vn != "?" && elem != vn {
            self.error("cannot assign " + vn + " to array element of type " + elem);
        }
        return ty_from_name(vn);
    }

    fn check_field_access(&mut self, obj: Expr, field: String) -> Ty {
        let ot = self.check_expr(obj);
        let on = ty_name(ot);
        if on == "?" { return TyUnknown; }
        if is_array_type_name(on) {
            if field == "length" { return TyInt; }
            self.error("array has no field '" + field + "' (only 'length')");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        self.ensure_instantiated(on);
        let found = self.structs.get(on);
        if let Some { value: info } = found {
            let ft = find_field_type(info, field);
            if ft == "?" {
                self.error("struct '" + on + "' has no field '" + field + "'");
                return TyUnknown;
            }
            return ty_from_name(ft);
        } else {
            self.error("type '" + on + "' has no field '" + field + "'");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
    }

    fn check_field_assign(&mut self, obj: Expr, field: String, value: Expr) -> Ty {
        let ot = self.check_expr(obj);
        let on = ty_name(ot);
        let vt = self.check_expr(value);
        let vn = ty_name(vt);
        if on == "?" { return ty_from_name(vn); }
        self.ensure_instantiated(on);
        let found = self.structs.get(on);
        if let Some { value: info } = found {
            let ft = find_field_type(info, field);
            if ft == "?" {
                self.error("struct '" + on + "' has no field '" + field + "'");
            } else if vn != "?" && ft != vn {
                self.error("cannot assign " + vn + " to field '" + field + "' of type " + ft);
            }
        } else {
            self.error("type '" + on + "' has no field '" + field + "'");
        }
        return ty_from_name(vn);
    }

    // `name` may be a real struct's own name, or an enum variant's name -- resolved to whichever
    // name the field table is actually registered under (the enum's own name for a variant, see
    // `register_enum`) so the returned `Ty` is always the *constructible* type's real name.
    fn check_struct_lit(&mut self, name: String, field_names: Vec<String>, field_values: Vec<Expr>, generic_base: Option<String>, expected_mono: Option<String>) -> Ty {
        var type_name = name;
        // An explicitly-qualified generic-enum-variant construction (`Option<Int>::Some { ... }`)
        // already names its exact concrete instantiation -- skip the bare-name `variant_owner`
        // lookup entirely (see `Ast.hc`'s own `EnumDecl` header for why that lookup alone can't
        // disambiguate once a generic enum is used at more than one type argument).
        var is_qualified = false;
        if let Some { value: base } = generic_base {
            type_name = base;
            is_qualified = true;
        }
        if !is_qualified {
            // A `variant_owner` entry is only trustworthy here when its OWNER is itself a
            // non-mono name -- a real, hand-declared non-generic enum's own name never contains
            // "$" (e.g. a program declaring its OWN `enum Opt2 { Some {...}, None {} }` alongside
            // the always-injected prelude's `Option<T>`, real `demo_program34.hcsrc` usage: both
            // enums use the SAME variant names "Some"/"None"). When the owner IS a mono name
            // ("Option$Expr"), it's a SIDE EFFECT of whichever concrete instantiation happened to
            // monomorphize FIRST (`ensure_instantiated` -> `register_enum(mono_name, ...)`, the
            // same non-generic registration path a real one-shot enum uses) -- once that happens,
            // every LATER bare `Some { ... }`/`None` site would silently resolve to that first
            // instantiation's owner forever, regardless of what type argument this particular
            // site actually needs (real gap found self-compiling `Parser.hotc`, which constructs
            // `Option<T>` at several different `T`s: `Option<Expr>`, `Option<Vec<Stmt>>`,
            // `Option<String>`) -- `generic_variant_owner` (registered once at DECLARATION time,
            // never mutated) is checked instead in that case; `type_name` becomes the generic
            // BASE ("Option"), resolved to the real mono name by the block right below. If
            // NEITHER resolves cleanly, falls back to the (possibly stale) `variant_owner` entry
            // anyway -- better than nothing, preserves this lookup's original behavior for
            // whatever edge case neither table above was meant to cover.
            var resolved = false;
            let owner_found = self.variant_owner.get(name);
            if let Some { value: owner } = owner_found {
                if !is_mono_name(owner) {
                    type_name = owner;
                    resolved = true;
                }
            }
            if !resolved {
                let generic_owner_found = self.generic_variant_owner.get(name);
                if let Some { value: gowner } = generic_owner_found {
                    type_name = gowner;
                    resolved = true;
                }
            }
            if !resolved {
                let owner_found2 = self.variant_owner.get(name);
                if let Some { value: owner2 } = owner_found2 {
                    type_name = owner2;
                }
            }
        }
        // A bare generic struct/enum literal ("Vec { ... }" / "Some { ... }", no "<Arg>") relying
        // on the surrounding `return` statement's own declared return type to supply the missing
        // type argument -- e.g. `return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };` inside a fn declared `->
        // Vec<FnDecl>`, or `return Some { value: e };` inside a fn declared `-> Option<Expr>`. A
        // real, disclosed-scope gap found self-compiling `Parser.hotc`, whose own helper fns
        // (`empty_fn_decl_vec`, `some_expr`, etc) all follow exactly this idiom. Scope cut: ONLY
        // return-position inference (the only shape any real self-hosted source actually needs,
        // see `check_expr`'s own `StructLit` dispatch, which passes `self.cur_ret_ty`
        // unconditionally as `expected_mono`) -- `let`/`var` still have no declared-type
        // annotation to infer from at all (this subset's own `let`/`var` are always inferred from
        // `init`, never stored).
        if !is_qualified {
            var is_generic_base = false;
            let base_is_generic_struct = self.generic_templates.get(type_name);
            if let Some { value: bs } = base_is_generic_struct { is_generic_base = true; }
            let base_is_generic_enum = self.generic_enum_templates.get(type_name);
            if let Some { value: be } = base_is_generic_enum { is_generic_base = true; }
            if is_generic_base {
                var resolved_via_mono = false;
                if let Some { value: mono } = expected_mono {
                    if is_mono_name(mono) && mono_base(mono) == type_name {
                        type_name = mono;
                        resolved_via_mono = true;
                    }
                }
                // Fallback: the "Vec { data: someVar.data, len: 0 }" idiom -- an "empty, same-
                // element-type Vec" built by reusing an ALREADY-typed local's own backing array,
                // instead of via a dedicated `empty_*_vec`-style helper fn in return position
                // (which the block above already handles). Real gap found self-compiling
                // `Checker.hotc`'s own `checker_new`, which constructs several fields exactly this
                // way (`var mono = vec_of("__init__"); var mono_needed = Vec { data: mono.data,
                // len: 0 };`) -- a `var`/`let` binding has no declared-type annotation to infer
                // from at all in this subset (see this fn's own header), so a "data" field whose
                // value is LITERALLY `someIdent.data` is the next best signal: look up
                // `someIdent`'s own already-known type directly (a registry read, not a
                // `check_expr` call -- calling `check_expr` on the SAME sub-expression twice, once
                // here and once in the normal per-field validation loop below, would risk a
                // "use of moved value" on a non-Copy type, see this file's own header on that trap).
                if !resolved_via_mono {
                    var di = 0;
                    while di < field_names.length() {
                        if field_names.get(di) == "data" {
                            let fv = field_values.get(di);
                            match fv {
                                FieldAccess { obj, field } => {
                                    if field == "data" {
                                        match obj {
                                            Ident { name: oname } => {
                                                let ovar = self.vars.get(oname);
                                                if let Some { value: oty } = ovar {
                                                    let on = ty_name(oty);
                                                    if is_mono_name(on) && mono_base(on) == type_name {
                                                        type_name = on;
                                                    }
                                                }
                                            }
                                            _ => {}
                                        }
                                    }
                                }
                                _ => {}
                            }
                        }
                        di = di + 1;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        self.ensure_instantiated(type_name);
        let found = self.structs.get(type_name);
        if let Some { value: info } = found {
            // Prefer the CONSTRUCTED VARIANT's own dedicated field table over the enum-wide union
            // `info` (see `variant_field_info`'s own header) whenever `name` names a real variant
            // -- falls back to `info` for a plain (non-enum) struct, where there's only ever one
            // table anyway.
            var field_info = info;
            let variant_info = self.variant_field_info.get(type_name + "#" + name);
            if let Some { value: vinfo } = variant_info {
                field_info = vinfo;
            }
            var i = 0;
            while i < field_values.length() {
                let fname = field_names.get(i);
                let fty = self.check_expr(field_values.get(i));
                let fn_ = ty_name(fty);
                let expected = find_field_type(field_info, fname);
                if expected == "?" {
                    self.error("'" + name + "' has no field '" + fname + "'");
                } else if fn_ != "?" && expected != fn_ {
                    self.error("field '" + fname + "' of '" + name + "' expects " + expected + ", got " + fn_);
                }
                i = i + 1;
            }
            return TyStruct { name: type_name };
        } else {
            self.error("unknown struct or enum variant '" + name + "'");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
    }

    // Called once per `StructDecl` (by the driver, before any fn body is checked -- mirrors
    // `fn_sigs`' own forward-registration so field types resolve regardless of declaration
    // order). Declared field types are trusted at face value the same way a fn param's declared
    // type is -- see `ty_from_name`. A *generic* struct (`type_param` is `Some`) goes into
    // `generic_templates` instead -- it isn't directly usable until `ensure_instantiated`
    // monomorphizes some concrete `Base$Arg` name for it.
    fn register_struct(&mut self, name: String, type_param: Option<String>, fields: Vec<Param>) {
        var names = empty_str_vec2();
        var types = empty_str_vec2();
        var i = 0;
        while i < fields.length() {
            let f = fields.get(i);
            names.push(f.name);
            types.push(f.type_name);
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if let Some { value: tp } = type_param {
            self.generic_templates.register(name, GenericTemplate { type_param: tp, field_names: names, field_types: types });
        } else {
            self.structs.register(name, StructInfo { field_names: names, field_types: types });
        }
    }

    // Called once per `ExternClassDecl` (mirrors `register_struct`/`register_enum`). Declared
    // signatures are trusted at face value -- no real classpath reflection this phase (see
    // `Ast.hc`'s own `ExternClassDecl` header), the same "declared type is truth" honesty every
    // other declared type already gets in this checker.
    fn register_extern(&mut self, name: String, binary_name: String, methods: Vec<ExternMethod>) {
        self.externs.register(name, binary_name);
        var i = 0;
        while i < methods.length() {
            let m = methods.get(i);
            let key = name + "#" + m.name;
            self.extern_methods.register(key, ExternMethodInfo { param_types: param_type_names_of4(m.params), ret_type: m.ret_type, is_static: m.is_static, is_ctor: m.is_ctor });
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }

    // Called once per `StaticDecl` (mirrors `register_struct`/`register_extern`'s own forward-
    // registration, done before any fn body is checked so a static's declaration order relative to
    // its uses never matters) -- the declared `type_name` is trusted at face value, same "declared
    // type is truth" honesty every other declared type already gets in this checker (this file
    // never actually evaluates `s.value` against it -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `StaticDecl` header).
    fn register_static_decl(&mut self, s: StaticDecl) {
        self.statics.register(s.name, ty_from_name(s.type_name));
    }

    // If `name` is a monomorphized instantiation (`"Box$Int"`) not yet concretely registered,
    // substitutes its generic template's type parameter with the real argument and registers the
    // result as an ordinary struct under `name` -- after this call, `name` behaves exactly like
    // any real, non-generic struct everywhere else in this file. Idempotent (checks `self.structs`
    // first) and records `name` into `mono_needed` on first instantiation only. Cascades: if a
    // substituted field type is itself a *different* generic instantiation (`"Vec$Int"`, from a
    // declared field like `Registry<T> { entries: Vec<T> }`), that nested instantiation gets
    // `ensure_instantiated`'d right here too -- otherwise `"Vec$Int"` would sit in `Registry$Int`'s
    // own field table naming a class that never actually got emitted, a real
    // `NoClassDefFoundError` waiting to happen at runtime the moment the field was ever accessed
    // by code this checker hadn't *also* happened to check some other way (see
    // `substitute_field_type`'s own header for the plain substitution half of this).
    fn ensure_instantiated(&mut self, name: String) {
        if !is_mono_name(name) { return; }
        let already = self.structs.get(name);
        var exists = false;
        if let Some { value: x } = already { exists = true; }
        if exists { return; }
        let base = mono_base(name);
        let arg = mono_arg(name);
        let tmpl_found = self.generic_templates.get(base);
        if let Some { value: tmpl } = tmpl_found {
            var fields = empty_param_vec3();
            var i = 0;
            while i < tmpl.field_names.length() {
                let ftype = substitute_field_type(tmpl.field_types.get(i), tmpl.type_param, arg);
                let ctarget = cascade_target(ftype);
                if is_mono_name(ctarget) {
                    self.ensure_instantiated(ctarget);
                }
                fields.push(Param { name: tmpl.field_names.get(i), type_name: ftype });
                i = i + 1;
            }
            self.register_struct(name, none_string3(), fields);
            self.mono_needed.push(name);
            return;
        }
        let etmpl_found = self.generic_enum_templates.get(base);
        if let Some { value: etmpl } = etmpl_found {
            var new_variants = empty_variant_vec3();
            var vi2 = 0;
            while vi2 < etmpl.variants.length() {
                let v = etmpl.variants.get(vi2);
                var new_fields = empty_param_vec3();
                var fi3 = 0;
                while fi3 < v.fields.length() {
                    let f = v.fields.get(fi3);
                    let ftype2 = substitute_field_type(f.type_name, etmpl.type_param, arg);
                    let ctarget2 = cascade_target(ftype2);
                    if is_mono_name(ctarget2) {
                        self.ensure_instantiated(ctarget2);
                    }
                    new_fields.push(Param { name: f.name, type_name: ftype2 });
                    fi3 = fi3 + 1;
                }
                new_variants.push(EnumVariant { name: v.name, fields: new_fields });
                vi2 = vi2 + 1;
            }
            self.register_enum(name, none_string3(), new_variants);
            self.mono_needed.push(name);
        }
    }

    // If `name` is a monomorphized FUNCTION instantiation (`"identity$Int"`) not yet concretely
    // registered, substitutes its generic template's type parameter into every param's declared
    // type (and the return type), registers the concrete signature under `name`, and re-checks a
    // substituted-params-AND-BODY copy of the template's own body against `name` (reusing
    // `check_fn` directly, same as a static impl method does) -- after this call, `name` behaves
    // exactly like any real, non-generic fn everywhere else in this file (`check_call` looks it up
    // in `fn_sigs`). Idempotent and records `name` into `fn_mono_needed` on first instantiation
    // only, mirroring `ensure_instantiated`. The body itself is walked too now (`subst_stmt_list`,
    // closing a gap disclosed earlier): a generic fn's body CAN reference its own still-abstract
    // `T` inside a nested generic construction site (`vec_of<T>`'s own `return Vec<T> { data:
    // [first; 4], len: 1 };`, say) -- that gets fixed up to the real concrete instantiation before
    // this substituted copy is checked, so `check_struct_lit`'s own `ensure_instantiated` call
    // cascades into the nested struct/enum instantiation exactly like it would for hand-written
    // concrete source, no special-casing needed here beyond the fix-up itself.
    fn ensure_fn_instantiated(&mut self, name: String) {
        if !is_mono_name(name) { return; }
        let already = self.fn_sigs.get(name);
        var exists = false;
        if let Some { value: s } = already { exists = true; }
        if exists { return; }
        let base = mono_base(name);
        let arg = mono_arg(name);
        let tmpl_found = self.generic_fn_templates.get(base);
        if let Some { value: tmpl } = tmpl_found {
            var found_tp = "";
            if let Some { value: tp } = tmpl.type_param { found_tp = tp; }
            // Builds `new_param_tys` alongside `new_params` in the same pass -- rather than
            // deriving it afterward via `param_types_of(new_params)`, which would MOVE
            // `new_params` (it takes `Vec<Param>` by value) before `mono_decl` below needs it
            // again.
            var new_params = empty_param_vec3();
            var new_param_tys = empty_ty_vec();
            var pi = 0;
            while pi < tmpl.params.length() {
                let p = tmpl.params.get(pi);
                let pty = substitute_field_type(p.type_name, found_tp, arg);
                new_params.push(Param { name: p.name, type_name: pty });
                new_param_tys.push(ty_from_name(pty));
                pi = pi + 1;
            }
            let ret_ty_str = opt_string_or2(tmpl.ret_type, "Int");
            let new_ret = substitute_field_type(ret_ty_str, found_tp, arg);
            self.fn_sigs.register(name, FnSig { param_types: new_param_tys, ret_type: ty_from_name(new_ret) });
            self.fn_mono_needed.push(name);
            let substituted_body = subst_stmt_list(tmpl.body, found_tp, arg);
            var mono_decl = FnDecl { name: name, params: new_params, ret_type: some_string3(new_ret), body: substituted_body, type_param: none_string3() };
            // Real bug found building this: `ensure_fn_instantiated` can fire from the MIDDLE of
            // checking a totally different function's body (a call site inside it just triggered
            // the first-ever use of this instantiation) -- but `check_fn` unconditionally resets
            // `self.vars` to a fresh scope for whatever it's given, so calling it here without
            // saving/restoring first would wipe out every local the *caller's* own function had
            // already bound (a `let n = identity(37);` earlier in `combo`'s body, say), surfacing
            // as spurious "unknown variable" errors for names that were very much still in scope.
            // Save/restore around the nested check, same as any other re-entrant use of shared
            // mutable state.
            let saved_vars = self.vars;
            let saved_ret_ty = self.cur_ret_ty;
            self.check_fn(mono_decl);
            self.vars = saved_vars;
            self.cur_ret_ty = saved_ret_ty;
        }
    }

    // Called once per `EnumDecl` (mirrors `register_struct`). Registers the *union* of every
    // variant's fields under the enum's own name (a real, disclosed simplification -- field
    // lookups and `StructLit` validation don't distinguish which variant a field actually belongs
    // to, only whether it exists on *some* variant of this enum; see this file's own header), and
    // records each variant name -> this enum's name in `variant_owner` (bare lookup, unqualified
    // construction sites only) and `variant_owner_q` (qualified "Owner#Variant" existence check,
    // `check_match` reads this one). A *generic* enum (`type_param` is `Some`) goes into
    // `generic_enum_templates` instead, same as `register_struct`'s own generic branch -- it isn't
    // directly usable until `ensure_instantiated` monomorphizes a concrete `Base$Arg` name for it.
    fn register_enum(&mut self, name: String, type_param: Option<String>, variants: Vec<EnumVariant>) {
        // A single `if let ... { move variants; return; }` followed by code that also uses
        // `variants` trips the move-checker's conservative branch-merge behavior even though the
        // early `return` makes the two paths genuinely exclusive (it doesn't reason about
        // reachability past a `return`) -- restructured as one exhaustive `if`/`else`, each branch
        // using `variants` exactly once, mirroring `register_struct`'s own shape.
        var is_generic = false;
        var tp_val = "";
        if let Some { value: tp } = type_param {
            is_generic = true;
            tp_val = tp;
        }
        if is_generic {
            var gvi = 0;
            while gvi < variants.length() {
                let gv = variants.get(gvi);
                self.generic_variant_owner.register(gv.name, name);
                gvi = gvi + 1;
            }
            self.generic_enum_templates.register(name, GenericEnumTemplate { type_param: tp_val, variants: variants });
        } else {
            var names = empty_str_vec2();
            var types = empty_str_vec2();
            var variant_names = empty_str_vec2();
            var vi = 0;
            while vi < variants.length() {
                let v = variants.get(vi);
                self.variant_owner.register(v.name, name);
                self.variant_owner_q.register(name + "#" + v.name, true);
                variant_names.push(v.name);
                var v_names = empty_str_vec2();
                var v_types = empty_str_vec2();
                var fi = 0;
                while fi < v.fields.length() {
                    let f = v.fields.get(fi);
                    names.push(f.name);
                    types.push(f.type_name);
                    v_names.push(f.name);
                    v_types.push(f.type_name);
                    fi = fi + 1;
                }
                // Keyed "OwnerName#VariantName" (mirrors `variant_owner_q`), NOT bare `v.name` --
                // a bare key would collide across multiple concrete instantiations of the same
                // GENERIC enum (`Option$Expr#Some`'s `value: Expr` vs `Option$String#Some`'s
                // `value: String`, both registered under this same enum's own template, each via
                // its own `ensure_instantiated` -> `register_enum(mono_name, ...)` call), the
                // exact same collision already disclosed for `variant_owner` itself (see
                // `build_program_info`'s own header in `Codegen.hotc`) -- found self-compiling
                // `Parser.hotc`, whose real source constructs `Option<T>` at several different T's.
                self.variant_field_info.register(name + "#" + v.name, StructInfo { field_names: v_names, field_types: v_types });
                vi = vi + 1;
            }
            self.structs.register(name, StructInfo { field_names: names, field_types: types });
            self.enum_variants.register(name, variant_names);
        }
    }

    // A LOCAL (`self.vars`, reset fresh per fn/method) is checked first, so a local can still
    // shadow a top-level `static` of the same name -- only falls back to `self.statics` (global,
    // never reset) when there's no local by this name. See `Ast.hc`'s own `StaticDecl` header.
    fn check_ident(&mut self, name: String) -> Ty {
        let found = self.vars.get(name);
        if let Some { value: t } = found {
            return t;
        }
        let static_found = self.statics.get(name);
        if let Some { value: st } = static_found {
            return st;
        }
        // Neither a local nor a static -- a BARE fieldless-enum-variant reference used as an
        // ordinary value expression (real `Lexer.hotc`/`Parser.hotc` usage: `Token { kind: FN }`,
        // `kind == RETURN`, treating a fieldless enum's variants like plain named constants; also
        // covers real `Parser.hotc`'s own bare `return None;`, a GENERIC fieldless variant --
        // `self.cur_ret_ty` supplies the missing type argument, same as `check_expr`'s own
        // `StructLit` dispatch). Delegates straight to `check_struct_lit` with empty field lists --
        // exactly what `Name {}` would check, reusing that logic rather than duplicating it (and
        // inheriting its existing, disclosed imprecision: neither path verifies every declared
        // field was actually supplied, so this doesn't introduce a new gap, just extends an old
        // one to a new syntax form).
        let variant_found = self.variant_owner.get(name);
        let generic_variant_found = self.generic_variant_owner.get(name);
        var is_known_variant = false;
        if let Some { value: owner } = variant_found { is_known_variant = true; }
        if let Some { value: gowner } = generic_variant_found { is_known_variant = true; }
        if is_known_variant {
            return self.check_struct_lit(name, empty_str_vec2(), empty_expr_vec3(), none_string3(), some_string3(self.cur_ret_ty));
        }
        self.error("unknown variable '" + name + "'");
        return TyUnknown;
    }

    fn check_binary(&mut self, op: String, left: Expr, right: Expr) -> Ty {
        let lt = self.check_expr(left);
        let rt = self.check_expr(right);
        let ln = ty_name(lt);
        let rn = ty_name(rt);
        if op == "+" && ln == "String" && rn == "String" {
            return TyStr;
        } else if op == "+" || op == "-" || op == "*" || op == "/" || op == "%" {
            if ln == "Int" && rn == "Int" {
                return TyInt;
            } else if ln == "Long" && rn == "Long" {
                // Both operands must be `Long` -- no implicit `Int` -> `Long` widening this round
                // (a real, disclosed scope cut; `Codegen.hotc`'s own `gen_binary` enforces the
                // same restriction, since `LADD`/etc. need BOTH operands already category-2).
                return TyLong;
            } else if ln == "?" || rn == "?" {
                return TyUnknown;
            } else {
                self.error("'" + op + "' needs Int or Long operands (both the same), got " + ln + " and " + rn);
                return TyUnknown;
            }
        } else if op == "&&" || op == "||" {
            if ln == "Bool" && rn == "Bool" {
                return TyBool;
            } else if ln == "?" || rn == "?" {
                return TyUnknown;
            } else {
                self.error("'" + op + "' needs Bool operands, got " + ln + " and " + rn);
                return TyUnknown;
            }
        } else if op == "<" || op == "<=" || op == ">" || op == ">=" {
            if (ln == "Int" && rn == "Int") || (ln == "Long" && rn == "Long") || ln == "?" || rn == "?" {
                return TyBool;
            } else {
                self.error("'" + op + "' needs Int or Long operands (both the same), got " + ln + " and " + rn);
                return TyBool;
            }
        } else {
            // "==" / "!=" -- always produce Bool; real structural-vs-reference-equality
            // subtleties (see IDEAS.md) are left to codegen, not this checker's concern.
            return TyBool;
        }
    }

    fn check_unary(&mut self, op: String, inner: Expr) -> Ty {
        let it = self.check_expr(inner);
        let iname = ty_name(it);
        if op == "!" {
            if iname == "Bool" || iname == "?" {
                return TyBool;
            } else {
                self.error("'!' needs a Bool operand, got " + iname);
                return TyBool;
            }
        } else {
            if iname == "Int" || iname == "?" {
                return TyInt;
            } else {
                self.error("unary '-' needs an Int operand, got " + iname);
                return TyInt;
            }
        }
    }

    fn check_call(&mut self, callee: String, args: Vec<Expr>) -> Ty {
        var arg_tys = self.check_arg_list(args);
        let found = self.fn_sigs.get(callee);
        if let Some { value: sig } = found {
            let expected_count = sig.param_types.length();
            let got_count = arg_tys.length();
            if expected_count != got_count {
                self.error("'" + callee + "' expects " + JString::valueOf(expected_count) + " args, got " + JString::valueOf(got_count));
            } else {
                var i = 0;
                while i < got_count {
                    let expected = sig.param_types.get(i);
                    let got = arg_tys.get(i);
                    let en = ty_name(expected);
                    let gn = ty_name(got);
                    if en != "?" && gn != "?" && en != gn {
                        self.error("argument " + JString::valueOf(i + 1) + " to '" + callee + "' expects " + en + ", got " + gn);
                    }
                    i = i + 1;
                }
            }
            return sig.ret_type;
        } else {
            let gtmpl_found = self.generic_fn_templates.get(callee);
            if let Some { value: gtmpl } = gtmpl_found {
                return self.check_generic_call(callee, gtmpl, arg_tys);
            }
            // Unknown callee (e.g. a real builtin like `print` this phase's symbol table
            // doesn't know about) -- trust it, same spirit as `extern class`'s own "no
            // verification, trust the call site" honesty elsewhere in this compiler.
            return TyUnknown;
        }
    }

    // Infers a generic top-level fn's type argument from whichever argument's corresponding
    // declared param type is EXACTLY the bare type parameter (`fn identity<T>(x: T)` -- `x`'s
    // declared type is literally `"T"`, so the arg passed for `x` gives the concrete type
    // directly) -- a real, disclosed scope cut: doesn't infer through `"[T]"` or a nested generic
    // argument, only this one bare-match rule (see `Ast.hc`'s own `FnDecl` header). Builds the
    // mono name (`"identity$Int"`), `ensure_fn_instantiated`s it, and returns its real (now-
    // concrete) return type.
    fn check_generic_call(&mut self, callee: String, gtmpl: FnDecl, arg_tys: Vec<Ty>) -> Ty {
        var found_tp = "";
        if let Some { value: tp } = gtmpl.type_param { found_tp = tp; }
        var inferred = "";
        var found_infer = false;
        var i = 0;
        while i < gtmpl.params.length() {
            let p = gtmpl.params.get(i);
            if p.type_name == found_tp && i < arg_tys.length() {
                inferred = ty_name(arg_tys.get(i));
                found_infer = true;
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if !found_infer || inferred == "?" {
            self.error("could not infer generic type argument for call to '" + callee + "'");
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        let mono_name = callee + "$" + inferred;
        self.ensure_fn_instantiated(mono_name);
        let sig_found = self.fn_sigs.get(mono_name);
        if let Some { value: sig } = sig_found {
            return sig.ret_type;
        }
        return TyUnknown;
    }

    // If `rn` is a mono struct instantiation (`"Box$Int"`) and `method` names an instance method
    // declared in the generic struct's own `impl` block (`generic_method_templates`, keyed by the
    // BASE struct name -- `"Box#get"`) not yet concretely registered under `rn`'s own key, infers
    // `T` directly from `rn` itself (`mono_arg("Box$Int")` = `"Int"` -- no scanning needed, unlike
    // a generic top-level fn's own call-site inference, since a method's receiver already names
    // its own concrete instantiation), substitutes params/return/body, registers the concrete
    // `FnSig` under `rn + "#" + method`, and re-checks the substituted copy (`check_method`,
    // save/restore `self.vars` around it -- same re-entrancy concern `ensure_fn_instantiated`'s own
    // header explains). A no-op for anything that isn't this exact shape (a plain struct's own
    // method, `String`'s built-ins, a genuine typo) -- `check_method_call`'s own existing lookup
    // right after this call handles all of those unchanged.
    fn ensure_method_instantiated(&mut self, owner: String, method: String) {
        if !is_mono_name(owner) { return; }
        let key = owner + "#" + method;
        let already = self.methods.get(key);
        var exists = false;
        if let Some { value: s } = already { exists = true; }
        if exists { return; }
        let base = mono_base(owner);
        let arg = mono_arg(owner);
        let gm_found = self.generic_method_templates.get(base + "#" + method);
        if let Some { value: gm } = gm_found {
            var found_tp = "";
            let tmpl_found = self.generic_templates.get(base);
            if let Some { value: tmpl } = tmpl_found { found_tp = tmpl.type_param; }
            var new_params = empty_param_vec3();
            var new_param_tys = empty_ty_vec();
            var pi = 0;
            while pi < gm.params.length() {
                let p = gm.params.get(pi);
                let pty = substitute_field_type(p.type_name, found_tp, arg);
                new_params.push(Param { name: p.name, type_name: pty });
                new_param_tys.push(ty_from_name(pty));
                pi = pi + 1;
            }
            let ret_ty_str = opt_string_or2(gm.ret_type, "Int");
            let new_ret = substitute_field_type(ret_ty_str, found_tp, arg);
            self.methods.register(key, FnSig { param_types: new_param_tys, ret_type: ty_from_name(new_ret) });
            self.method_mono_needed.push(key);
            let substituted_body = subst_stmt_list(gm.body, found_tp, arg);
            var mono_decl = FnDecl { name: method, params: new_params, ret_type: some_string3(new_ret), body: substituted_body, type_param: none_string3() };
            let saved_vars = self.vars;
            let saved_ret_ty = self.cur_ret_ty;
            self.check_method(owner, mono_decl);
            self.vars = saved_vars;
            self.cur_ret_ty = saved_ret_ty;
        }
    }

    fn check_method_call(&mut self, recv: Expr, method: String, args: Vec<Expr>) -> Ty {
        let rt = self.check_expr(recv);
        let rn = ty_name(rt);
        var arg_tys = self.check_arg_list(args);
        if rn == "?" {
            return TyUnknown;
        }
        self.ensure_instantiated(rn);
        self.ensure_method_instantiated(rn, method);
        let key = rn + "#" + method;
        let extern_found = self.extern_methods.get(key);
        if let Some { value: m } = extern_found {
            let expected_count = m.param_types.length();
            let got_count = arg_tys.length();
            if expected_count != got_count {
                self.error("'" + method + "' expects " + JString::valueOf(expected_count) + " args, got " + JString::valueOf(got_count));
            } else {
                var i = 0;
                while i < got_count {
                    let expected = m.param_types.get(i);
                    let got = arg_tys.get(i);
                    let gn = ty_name(got);
                    if expected != "?" && gn != "?" && expected != gn {
                        self.error("argument " + JString::valueOf(i + 1) + " to '" + method + "' expects " + expected + ", got " + gn);
                    }
                    i = i + 1;
                }
            }
            if m.ret_type == "Self" { return ty_from_name(rn); }
            return ty_from_name(m.ret_type);
        }
        let found = self.methods.get(key);
        if let Some { value: sig } = found {
            let expected_count = sig.param_types.length();
            let got_count = arg_tys.length();
            if expected_count != got_count {
                self.error("'" + method + "' expects " + JString::valueOf(expected_count) + " args, got " + JString::valueOf(got_count));
            } else {
                var i = 0;
                while i < got_count {
                    let expected = sig.param_types.get(i);
                    let got = arg_tys.get(i);
                    let en = ty_name(expected);
                    let gn = ty_name(got);
                    if en != "?" && gn != "?" && en != gn {
                        self.error("argument " + JString::valueOf(i + 1) + " to '" + method + "' expects " + en + ", got " + gn);
                    }
                    i = i + 1;
                }
            }
            return sig.ret_type;
        } else {
            // No user-defined method by this name on this receiver type -- trust it (same
            // "unknown, don't verify" spirit as `check_call`'s own unknown-callee case). Covers
            // real receivers this checker has no method table for at all (`String`'s built-in
            // methods, resolved for real only in `Codegen.hotc`) as well as genuine typos, which
            // is a real, disclosed imprecision -- not distinguishing the two costs an error
            // message on a typo, but doesn't reject anything that should compile.
            return TyUnknown;
        }
    }

    fn check_assign(&mut self, name: String, value: Expr) -> Ty {
        let vt = self.check_expr(value);
        let vn = ty_name(vt);
        let existing = self.vars.get(name);
        if let Some { value: et } = existing {
            let en = ty_name(et);
            if en != "?" && vn != "?" && en != vn {
                self.error("cannot assign " + vn + " to '" + name + "' of type " + en);
            }
        } else {
            self.error("unknown variable '" + name + "'");
        }
        // `vt` was already consumed by `ty_name(vt)` above -- reconstruct fresh from the name
        // string instead of trying to reuse the (now-moved) original enum value.
        return ty_from_name(vn);
    }

    // === Statements ===

    fn check_stmt(&mut self, s: Stmt) {
        match s {
            Let { name, mutable, init } => {
                let t = self.check_expr(init);
                self.vars.register(name, t);
            }
            ExprStmt { expr } => {
                self.check_expr(expr);
            }
            Return { value } => {
                if let Some { value: v } = value {
                    self.check_expr(v);
                }
            }
            If { cond, then_body, else_body } => {
                let ct = self.check_expr(cond);
                let cn = ty_name(ct);
                if cn != "Bool" && cn != "?" {
                    self.error("if condition must be Bool, got " + cn);
                }
                self.check_block(then_body);
                if let Some { value: eb } = else_body {
                    self.check_block(eb);
                }
            }
            While { cond, body } => {
                let ct = self.check_expr(cond);
                let cn = ty_name(ct);
                if cn != "Bool" && cn != "?" {
                    self.error("while condition must be Bool, got " + cn);
                }
                self.loop_depth = self.loop_depth + 1;
                self.check_block(body);
                self.loop_depth = self.loop_depth - 1;
            }
            Match { subject, arms } => {
                self.check_match(subject, arms);
            }
            // `throw` now accepts either a plain `String` message (wrapped into a hardcoded
            // `java.lang.RuntimeException` at codegen, unchanged) OR an already-constructed real
            // exception object (`throw LexError::new(msg);` -- real `Lexer.hotc` usage, a genuine
            // gap found self-compiling it, since `extern class` support didn't exist AT ALL when
            // this restriction was first written) -- thrown directly via a real `ATHROW`, no
            // wrapping. `Int`/`Bool` are the only types that can never be legitimately thrown
            // either way; everything else (String, a struct, an extern-typed value) is trusted,
            // matching this checker's existing "no real classpath reflection, trust declared
            // types" honesty elsewhere (no verification that a non-String value is ACTUALLY a real
            // `Throwable` under the hood).
            Throw { value } => {
                let vt = self.check_expr(value);
                let vn = ty_name(vt);
                if vn == "Int" || vn == "Bool" {
                    self.error("throw needs a String message or a real exception value, got " + vn);
                }
            }
            // No drop-list tracking (`varsToDropBeforeBreak`/`varsToDropBeforeContinue` in the
            // real compiler) -- this subset has no `Drop`/RAII mechanism at all, so there's
            // nothing to clean up on the way out. `self.loop_depth` (incremented/decremented
            // around a `While`'s own body check, below) is the only thing that needs tracking.
            Break {} => {
                if self.loop_depth == 0 {
                    self.error("'break' outside of a loop");
                }
            }
            Continue {} => {
                if self.loop_depth == 0 {
                    self.error("'continue' outside of a loop");
                }
            }
            // `catch_type` must name a real, known `extern class` -- there's no other way this
            // subset has to name a real JVM exception type. `catch_var` is registered into
            // `self.vars` the same way a match-arm binding is (this subset's own vars aren't
            // block-scoped at all -- see this file's own header -- so it stays visible afterward
            // too, same as every other binding here).
            Try { try_block, catch_var, catch_type, catch_block } => {
                self.check_block(try_block);
                let ext_found = self.externs.get(catch_type);
                var found_ext = false;
                if let Some { value: bin } = ext_found { found_ext = true; }
                if found_ext {
                    self.vars.register(catch_var, TyStruct { name: catch_type });
                } else {
                    self.error("'catch' type '" + catch_type + "' is not a known extern class");
                }
                self.check_block(catch_block);
            }
        }
    }

    // No exhaustiveness checking this phase (a real, disclosed simplification -- the real
    // checker's own exhaustiveness check is a substantially bigger feature than binding/type
    // validation alone). Each arm's bound field names are validated against the *enum's* union
    // field table (see `register_enum`'s own header), not the specific variant's own fields --
    // same simplification `StructLit` construction already makes.
    fn check_match(&mut self, subject: Expr, arms: Vec<MatchArm>) {
        let st = self.check_expr(subject);
        let sn = ty_name(st);
        // A generic enum instantiation might only ever be *matched* in this particular function
        // (constructed elsewhere, possibly checked later -- fn declaration order doesn't imply
        // check order dependencies should exist at all), so unlike a plain enum's already-real
        // `structs`/`enum_variants`/`variant_owner_q` entries, `sn` might not be registered yet.
        // Real bug found building this: `check_field_access`/`check_method_call` already call
        // `ensure_instantiated` on their receiver's type for exactly this reason -- `check_match`
        // was missing the same call, so a generic-enum-typed match subject with no construction
        // site checked earlier in the same run failed every arm with a spurious "'Some' is not a
        // variant of 'Opt$Int'".
        self.ensure_instantiated(sn);
        var has_wildcard = false;
        var covered = empty_str_vec2();
        var i = 0;
        while i < arms.length() {
            let arm = arms.get(i);
            if arm.variant_name == "_" {
                has_wildcard = true;
            } else {
                covered.push(arm.variant_name);
                // `variant_owner_q` (a qualified "Owner#Variant" existence table, populated for
                // every enum -- plain and generic mono instantiations alike) rather than the bare
                // `variant_owner` lookup, since the latter would collide across multiple concrete
                // instantiations of the same generic enum (see `Ast.hc`'s own `EnumDecl` header).
                let owner_q_found = self.variant_owner_q.get(sn + "#" + arm.variant_name);
                var owner_ok = false;
                if let Some { value: ok } = owner_q_found { owner_ok = true; }
                if !owner_ok && sn != "?" {
                    self.error("'" + arm.variant_name + "' is not a variant of '" + sn + "'");
                }
            }
            var fj = 0;
            while fj < arm.field_names.length() {
                let fname = arm.field_names.get(fj);
                let bname = arm.bind_names.get(fj);
                var fty = "?";
                // Prefer the ARM's OWN variant's dedicated field table (`variant_field_info`,
                // keyed "OwnerName#VariantName") over the enum-wide union `self.structs.get(sn)`
                // -- the same real gap `check_struct_lit` already had and fixed (see that fn's own
                // `variant_field_info` header): `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr` enum reuses the field name
                // "value" across many variants at DIFFERENT types (`IntLit.value: Int`,
                // `StringLit.value: String`, ...), so the union table's first-match-wins linear
                // scan always resolved every arm's "value" binding to whichever variant registered
                // first (`IntLit`'s `Int`) -- a real bug found self-compiling `Checker.hotc`'s own
                // `check_match`, silently mistyping every OTHER variant's bindings until this.
                // Falls back to the union table when there's no per-variant entry (a plain,
                // non-enum struct never being matched this way in practice, but harmless either
                // way).
                let variant_info_found = self.variant_field_info.get(sn + "#" + arm.variant_name);
                if let Some { value: vinfo } = variant_info_found {
                    fty = find_field_type(vinfo, fname);
                } else {
                    let info_found = self.structs.get(sn); // fresh every iteration -- see check_match's own header
                    if let Some { value: info } = info_found {
                        fty = find_field_type(info, fname);
                    }
                }
                self.vars.register(bname, ty_from_name(fty));
                fj = fj + 1;
            }
            self.check_block(arm.body);
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if !has_wildcard && sn != "?" {
            let variants_found = self.enum_variants.get(sn);
            if let Some { value: variants } = variants_found {
                var vi = 0;
                while vi < variants.length() {
                    let vname = variants.get(vi);
                    var found_cov = false;
                    var ci = 0;
                    while ci < covered.length() {
                        if covered.get(ci) == vname { found_cov = true; }
                        ci = ci + 1;
                    }
                    if !found_cov {
                        self.error("match on '" + sn + "' is not exhaustive, missing variant '" + vname + "'");
                    }
                    vi = vi + 1;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    fn check_block(&mut self, stmts: Vec<Stmt>) {
        var i = 0;
        while i < stmts.length() {
            self.check_stmt(stmts.get(i));
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }

    fn check_arg_list(&mut self, args: Vec<Expr>) -> Vec<Ty> {
        if args.length() == 0 {
            return empty_ty_vec();
        }
        var out = vec_of(self.check_expr(args.get(0)));
        var i = 1;
        while i < args.length() {
            out.push(self.check_expr(args.get(i)));
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return out;
    }

    // === Top level ===

    // Called once per impl-block method (by the driver, before any fn/method body is checked --
    // mirrors `fn_sigs`'/`register_struct`'s own forward-registration).
    fn register_method(&mut self, struct_name: String, f: FnDecl) {
        let key = struct_name + "#" + f.name;
        self.methods.register(key, FnSig { param_types: param_types_of(f.params), ret_type: ty_from_name(opt_string_or2(f.ret_type, "Int")) });
    }

    // Same as `register_method`, but into `static_methods` -- no `self` to worry about, plain
    // params like a top-level fn's own `fn_sigs` registration.
    fn register_static_method(&mut self, struct_name: String, f: FnDecl) {
        let key = struct_name + "#" + f.name;
        self.static_methods.register(key, FnSig { param_types: param_types_of(f.params), ret_type: ty_from_name(opt_string_or2(f.ret_type, "Int")) });
    }

    // Like `check_fn`, but also binds `self` (the struct's own type) into the variable
    // environment before checking params/body -- `self` isn't itself an entry in `f.params` (see
    // `Ast.hc`'s `ImplDecl` header).
    fn check_method(&mut self, struct_name: String, f: FnDecl) {
        // Fresh scope per fn/method -- `Registry<T>.get()` returns the *first* match (see this
        // file's own header), so without resetting here, a param name reused across two
        // functions with *different* types (two different fns both taking a param called `b`,
        // say) would silently resolve the second fn's own `b` to the first fn's leftover type.
        // Real bug, caught by the generics demo -- `combo`'s own `b: Int` was shadowed by an
        // earlier-checked fn's unrelated `b: Box$Int` until this fix.
        self.vars = registry_new("__init__", TyUnknown);
        self.vars.register("self", ty_from_name(struct_name));
        self.cur_ret_ty = opt_string_or2(f.ret_type, "Unit");
        var i = 0;
        while i < f.params.length() {
            let p = f.params.get(i);
            self.vars.register(p.name, ty_from_name(p.type_name));
            i = i + 1;
        }
        self.check_block(f.body);
    }

    fn check_fn(&mut self, f: FnDecl) {
        self.vars = registry_new("__init__", TyUnknown); // fresh scope per fn -- see check_method's own comment
        self.cur_ret_ty = opt_string_or2(f.ret_type, "Unit");
        var i = 0;
        while i < f.params.length() {
            let p = f.params.get(i);
            self.vars.register(p.name, ty_from_name(p.type_name));
            i = i + 1;
        }
        self.check_block(f.body);
    }

    // Canonical "what real JVM type is this, for signature-matching purposes" key for a DECLARED
    // (HC-level) type name -- mirrors the real compiler's own `realTyKey` (see `JavaReflect.kt`'s
    // header comment for why this port exists: the self-hosted subset had NO real classpath
    // reflection at all, only "trust the declared signature" honesty). `"?"` means
    // "unrepresentable for this comparison" (an HC-native struct/enum, matching the real
    // compiler's own `null` sentinel there) -- `verify_extern_signature` skips the whole check
    // when it sees this, rather than risk a false-positive mismatch for a shape this port doesn't
    // understand. `self.externs` binary names are stored EXACTLY as declared (dot or slash form
    // alike -- only `Codegen.hotc`'s own `dot_to_slash_cg` normalizes them, at bytecode-emission
    // time, not here), so this converts dot-to-slash itself before building the key, to compare
    // correctly against `real_method_key`'s own always-slash-form (`Class.getName()` normalized).
    fn declared_type_to_key(&mut self, type_name: String) -> String {
        if type_name == "Int" { return "I"; }
        if type_name == "Long" { return "J"; }
        if type_name == "Bool" { return "Z"; }
        if type_name == "Unit" { return "V"; }
        if type_name == "String" { return "Ljava/lang/String;"; }
        if is_array_type_name(type_name) {
            return "[" + self.declared_type_to_key(array_elem_type_name(type_name));
        }
        let bin_found = self.externs.get(type_name);
        if let Some { value: bin } = bin_found {
            return "L" + dot_to_slash_reflect(bin) + ";";
        }
        return "?";
    }

    // Real gap found trying to give `Checker.hotc` real classpath reflection at all: an extern
    // method's DECLARED signature was never checked against the real class it names -- a wrong
    // return type or param type compiled clean and only surfaced as a runtime `NoSuchMethodError`.
    // Deliberately narrower than the real compiler's own `verifyExternSignatures`: only the
    // explicit-signature form (this subset's ONLY extern-method form -- no `use { ... }`, no lazy
    // resolution, no `Ty.Dyn` bound-interface params) needs porting at all. NOT wired into
    // `register_extern`'s own automatic flow (that would need a real `--classpath` threaded all
    // the way from the CLI through `Driver.hotc`, which this subset's driver doesn't do yet) --
    // callable directly wherever a caller already has a real classpath available.
    fn verify_extern_signature(&mut self, alias_name: String, binary_name: String, method_name: String, param_type_names: Vec<String>, ret_type_name: String, is_static: Bool, is_ctor: Bool) {
        if !class_exists_reflect(binary_name) { return; } // not on this classpath -- nothing to check against
        let real_methods = resolve_real_methods(binary_name, method_name, is_ctor);
        if real_methods.length() == 0 {
            self.error("extern class '" + alias_name + "': no public member '" + method_name + "' found on '" + binary_name + "' on the configured classpath");
            return;
        }
        var declared_keys = empty_str_vec2();
        var any_unknown = false;
        var i = 0;
        while i < param_type_names.length() {
            let k = self.declared_type_to_key(param_type_names.get(i));
            if k == "?" { any_unknown = true; }
            declared_keys.push(k);
            i = i + 1;
        }
        var ret_key = "V";
        if !is_ctor {
            ret_key = self.declared_type_to_key(ret_type_name);
            if ret_key == "?" { any_unknown = true; }
        }
        // Any unrepresentable type means this declaration could never correspond to a real Java
        // member anyway -- skip rather than guess, same "degrade to no check" reasoning
        // `declared_type_to_key`'s own header already uses.
        if any_unknown { return; }
        var matched = false;
        var j = 0;
        while j < real_methods.length() {
            let rm = real_methods.get(j);
            if rm.is_static == is_static && str_vec_eq_reflect(rm.param_keys, declared_keys) {
                if is_ctor {
                    matched = true;
                } else if rm.ret_key == ret_key {
                    matched = true;
                }
            }
            j = j + 1;
        }
        if !matched {
            self.error("extern class '" + alias_name + "': declared '" + method_name + "' doesn't match any real overload of '" + binary_name + "." + method_name + "' on the configured classpath");
        }
    }
}

// === Real classpath reflection, ported from `JavaReflect.kt`'s `ClasspathReflector` ===
// The self-hosted subset had NO real classpath reflection at all before this -- `extern class`
// declarations were pure "trust me" honesty, matching this file's own long-standing header.
// `try`/`catch` (see `Ast.hc`'s own `Try` header) exists specifically to make this possible:
// `Class.forName` genuinely throws for a missing class, and there's no other way to turn that
// into "return false/empty" instead of crashing the whole compiler.

extern class JClassRefX = "java.lang.Class" {
    static fn forName(name: String) -> JClassRefX;
    fn getName(&self) -> String;
    fn isArray(&self) -> Bool;
    fn isPrimitive(&self) -> Bool;
    fn getComponentType(&self) -> JClassRefX;
    fn getMethods(&self) -> [JMethodRefX];
    fn getConstructors(&self) -> [JConstructorRefX];
}
extern class JMethodRefX = "java.lang.reflect.Method" {
    fn getName(&self) -> String;
    fn getParameterTypes(&self) -> [JClassRefX];
    fn getReturnType(&self) -> JClassRefX;
    fn getModifiers(&self) -> Int;
}
extern class JConstructorRefX = "java.lang.reflect.Constructor" {
    fn getParameterTypes(&self) -> [JClassRefX];
    fn getModifiers(&self) -> Int;
}
extern class JModifierRefX = "java.lang.reflect.Modifier" {
    static fn isPublic(mod: Int) -> Bool;
    static fn isStatic(mod: Int) -> Bool;
}
extern class JClassNotFoundRefX = "java.lang.ClassNotFoundException" {}

// One real, reflected overload -- `param_keys`/`ret_key` already in the same canonical-key form
// `declared_type_to_key` produces, so `verify_extern_signature` can compare them directly with a
// plain string/vec equality check.
pub struct ReflectedMethod {
    is_static: Bool,
    param_keys: Vec<String>,
    ret_key: String,
}

fn class_exists_reflect(binary_name: String) -> Bool {
    try {
        let c = JClassRefX::forName(binary_name);
        return true;
    } catch (e: JClassNotFoundRefX) {
        return false;
    }
}

// Same duplicated-helper pattern `Codegen.hotc`'s own `dot_to_slash_cg` already established
// (`_cg`-suffixed there to dodge the flat cross-module function namespace, `_reflect`-suffixed
// here for the same reason) -- `self.externs`' own binary names are stored exactly as declared
// (dot or slash form alike), but `real_method_key`'s own keys are always slash-form
// (`Class.getName()`, normalized the same way), so `declared_type_to_key` needs this too.
fn dot_to_slash_reflect(name: String) -> String {
    var out = "";
    var i = 0;
    while i < name.length() {
        let c = name.substring(i, i + 1);
        if c == "." {
            out = out + "/";
        } else {
            out = out + c;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

fn str_vec_eq_reflect(a: Vec<String>, b: Vec<String>) -> Bool {
    if a.length() != b.length() { return false; }
    var i = 0;
    while i < a.length() {
        if a.get(i) != b.get(i) { return false; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

// Mirrors the real compiler's own `javaClassToTyOrNull` + `realTyKey`, fused into one step since
// this port skips the intermediate `Ty` reconstruction entirely (no `Float`/`Double` support in
// this subset at all -- `isPrimitive()` without a name match below falls through to `"?"`,
// matching the real compiler's own "unrepresentable, skip" treatment for exactly those, plus
// `byte`/`short`/`char`). `Class.getName()` (not `==` against `Integer.TYPE`-style constants,
// which would need real static-FIELD reflection this subset doesn't have) is the only signal
// available for telling primitives apart -- it returns the primitive's own bare name ("int",
// "long", ...) for exactly those, so string-comparing it is both simpler and sufficient.
fn real_method_key(c: JClassRefX) -> String {
    if c.isArray() {
        let comp = c.getComponentType();
        return "[" + real_method_key(comp);
    }
    let name = c.getName();
    if name == "int" { return "I"; }
    if name == "long" { return "J"; }
    if name == "boolean" { return "Z"; }
    if name == "void" { return "V"; }
    if name == "java.lang.String" { return "Ljava/lang/String;"; }
    if c.isPrimitive() { return "?"; }
    return "L" + dot_to_slash_reflect(name) + ";";
}

fn empty_reflected_method_vec() -> Vec<ReflectedMethod> {
    var v = vec_of(ReflectedMethod { is_static: false, param_keys: empty_str_vec2(), ret_key: "?" });
    return Vec { data: v.data, len: 0 };
}

// `member_name` is ignored for a constructor lookup (`is_ctor`) -- every public constructor is a
// candidate, same as the real compiler's own `memberName == "new"` branch. The lone `catch`
// clause is never actually expected to fire here (`verify_extern_signature` already confirmed
// `class_exists_reflect(binary_name)` before calling this), but `try` still requires at least
// one -- same restriction this subset's own grammar puts on it, mirroring the real compiler's.
fn resolve_real_methods(binary_name: String, member_name: String, is_ctor: Bool) -> Vec<ReflectedMethod> {
    var out = empty_reflected_method_vec();
    try {
        let c = JClassRefX::forName(binary_name);
        if is_ctor {
            let ctors = c.getConstructors();
            var i = 0;
            while i < ctors.length {
                let ctor = ctors[i];
                let mods = ctor.getModifiers();
                if JModifierRefX::isPublic(mods) {
                    let ptypes = ctor.getParameterTypes();
                    var pkeys = empty_str_vec2();
                    var j = 0;
                    while j < ptypes.length {
                        pkeys.push(real_method_key(ptypes[j]));
                        j = j + 1;
                    }
                    out.push(ReflectedMethod { is_static: false, param_keys: pkeys, ret_key: "V" });
                }
                i = i + 1;
            }
        } else {
            let methods = c.getMethods();
            var i2 = 0;
            while i2 < methods.length {
                let m = methods[i2];
                let mods2 = m.getModifiers();
                if m.getName() == member_name && JModifierRefX::isPublic(mods2) {
                    let ptypes2 = m.getParameterTypes();
                    var pkeys2 = empty_str_vec2();
                    var j2 = 0;
                    while j2 < ptypes2.length {
                        pkeys2.push(real_method_key(ptypes2[j2]));
                        j2 = j2 + 1;
                    }
                    let rkey = real_method_key(m.getReturnType());
                    out.push(ReflectedMethod { is_static: JModifierRefX::isStatic(mods2), param_keys: pkeys2, ret_key: rkey });
                }
                i2 = i2 + 1;
            }
        }
    } catch (e2: JClassNotFoundRefX) {
    }
    return out;
}

